{
  "title": "At the pharmacy: it's my head that hurts, not me",
  "title_target": "В аптеці — V aptetsi",
  "goal": "Say which part of your body hurts, buy medicine, and understand why in Ukrainian it isn't you who hurts, but your head.",
  "bekal": [
    "name parts of the body",
    "use болить and болять correctly",
    "describe simple symptoms",
    "buy medicine and ask how to take it",
    "emergency sentences you should memorize"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Діалог — The pharmacy two blocks from the dorm",
      "intro": "Ani has a fever and goes to the pharmacy. Notice how her complaint is built: not \"I have a headache\" but \"at me, the head hurts\". The head is the subject.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Фармацевт:",
          "text": "[Доброго|DOB-ro-ho] [дня|dnya]. [Чим|Chim] [можу|MO-zhoo] [допомогти|do-po-moh-TI]?",
          "id": "Good afternoon. How can I help you?",
          "literal": "good — day. with-what — can — help?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[У|OO] [мене|MEH-neh] [болить|bo-LIT'] [голова|ho-lo-VA]. [І|EE] [горло|HOR-lo].",
          "id": "I have a headache. And a sore throat.",
          "literal": "at — me — hurts — head. and — throat."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Фармацевт:",
          "text": "[Температура|Tem-peh-ra-TOO-ra] [є|yeh]?",
          "id": "Do you have a fever?",
          "literal": "temperature — is-there?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Здається|Zda-YEH-tsya], [є|yeh]. [Тридцять|TRID-tsyat'] [сім|seem] [і|ee] [вісім|VEE-seem].",
          "id": "I think so. Thirty-seven point eight.",
          "literal": "it-seems, there-is. thirty — seven — and — eight."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Фармацевт:",
          "text": "[Давно|Dav-NO]?",
          "id": "For how long?",
          "literal": "long-ago?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Два|Dva] [дні|dnee].",
          "id": "Two days.",
          "literal": "two — days."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Фармацевт:",
          "text": "[Ось|Os'] [таблетки|tab-LET-ki] [від|veed] [голови|ho-lo-VI]. [І|EE] [чай|chai] [від|veed] [горла|HOR-la].",
          "id": "Here are tablets for the headache. And tea for your throat.",
          "literal": "here-are — tablets — against — head (genitive). and — tea — against — throat (genitive)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Як|Yak] [приймати|priy-MA-ti]?",
          "id": "How do I take them?",
          "literal": "how — to-take?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Фармацевт:",
          "text": "[Одну|Od-NOO] [таблетку|tab-LET-koo] [тричі|TRI-chee] [на|na] [день|den'], [після|PEES-lya] [їжі|YEE-zhee].",
          "id": "One tablet three times a day, after meals.",
          "literal": "one — tablet (accusative) — three-times — per — day, after — food (genitive)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Дякую|DYA-koo-yoo]. [А|A] [скільки|SKEEL'-ki] [коштує|KOSH-too-yeh]?",
          "id": "Thank you. And how much is it?",
          "literal": "thank-you. and — how-much — costs?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Фармацевт:",
          "text": "[Разом|RA-zom] [двісті|DVEES-tee] [тридцять|TRID-tsyat']. [Якщо|Yak-SHCHO] [не|neh] [стане|STA-neh] [краще|KRA-shcheh] — [до|do] [лікаря|LEE-ka-rya].",
          "id": "Two hundred thirty altogether. If you don't get better, see a doctor.",
          "literal": "together — two-hundred — thirty. if — not — becomes — better — to — doctor (genitive)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Ось|Os']. [Дякую|DYA-koo-yoo] [вам|vam]!",
          "id": "Here you are. Thank you!",
          "literal": "here-is. thank — to-you!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**У мене болить голова: the head is the subject.** Literally \"at me, the head hurts\". You don't do anything in this sentence; your body part does. This goes back to the *У мене є* pattern from Unit 4: the owner goes into родовий (genitive), and the thing becomes the subject.",
        "**Болить for one, болять for several.** *Болить голова* (one head), *болять зуби* (several teeth), *болять ноги* (two legs). ⚠️ The most common mistake: using *болить* for body parts that come in pairs.",
        "**Таблетки від голови literally means \"tablets from the head\".** The preposition **від** is used for medicine: *від голови*, *від горла*, *від кашлю*, *від температури*. It means \"against\", not \"from\" in the sense of origin. What follows it goes into the genitive.",
        "**Body temperature is said without a word for the decimal point.** *Тридцять сім і вісім* = 37.8. The word *і* replaces the point. In Ukraine 36.6 °C counts as normal, and from 37 people already call it a *температура*.",
        "**Приймати ліки, not пити ліки.** For medicine, the verb is *приймати* (to take). *Пити* is used only when the medicine really is a liquid. Notice that *приймати* also means \"to receive\": *приймати гостей* (to receive guests).",
        "**Тричі на день = three times a day.** Special forms: *раз* (once), *двічі* (twice), *тричі* (three times). From four times on, the usual forms are used: *чотири рази*, *п'ять разів*.",
        "**Якщо = if, as a condition.** Don't mix it up with *коли* (when). *Якщо не стане краще* (if you don't get better) versus *Коли стане краще* (when you get better). Two words that separate possibility from certainty."
      ],
